half the  residents died in their beds and at  least 28 staff have tested positive it's  a fantastic community and to see this  happening is heartbreaking it actually  is heartbreaking soon there were  multiple outbreaks and care homes across  the country most with a common story a  sudden onslaught of cases sometimes  dozens of dead testing and detection  falling woefully behind the wave  caregivers sick and off work those  remaining struggling to keep up in homes  that were often chronically understaffed  before this pandemic residents sharing  rooms and bathrooms staff lacking  personal protective equipment ideal  conditions for a virus to run rampant  those on the frontlines are mostly women  new immigrants and visible minorities  doing difficult deeply personal work for  low pay bathing feeding  changing adult diapers it can be  exhausting physically and emotionally  and they often work part-time in  multiple facilities to make ends meet it  remains at a critical low we really need  skilled workers in some care homes  there's been a collapse in frontline  staffing some sick some staying away  because the risk is too high in my  workplace we all drop like flies you  have no idea how fast it infects  everybody it's extremely like hard for  us because you grow attached to them you  don't want to refuse but obviously a lot  of people are gonna feel unsafe now  hundreds of homes are locked down  leaving families worried sick  agonizing at the unthinkable loved ones  left alone in their final hours here I  finally get in my father's on his last  breath literally dead lying in his bed  so I went for three weeks thinking my  father was fine he's not he's dead  and there's the harrowing accounts of  what happened inside Montreal's  residents heron the conditions beyond  words those who were still alive were  dehydrated and malnourished and hadn't  been bathed or changed in days most  staff had left a doctor arrived on the  scene so desperate she called her own  husband 